 entre industrial y synth-pop: armazón rítmico marcial y bailable al servicio de canciones fronterizas con el rock duro (pero con máquinas).","color":"#94a3b8","family":"Industrial y ruido","link":"https://volteret4.github.io/loops_timeline/#ebm"}],"book_tracks":[{"id":108626236877220,"artist":"Laibach","artist_id":112645987857966,"title":"Nova Akropola","year":1985,"ess":0,"yt":"tFH45PewVd4","kind":"a","book":{"id":8,"title":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","url":"https://volteret4.github.io/scaruffi/"},"scene":"Folk apocalíptico y escuela eslovena","scene_id":189309525519838,"scene_link":"https://volteret4.github.io/scaruffi/#apocalyptic-folk","chapters":["Sección 5.22"],"pages":[[1,212,null]]}],"mentions":[{"book":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","page":212,"text":"…music, film music and industrial music, the expressionistic school of Slovenia became the first relevant experience in Eastern Europe: Laibach, Autopsia (1), whose mid-life Kristallnacht (1994) was a gothic neo-classical symphony, Laibach (1), whose Nova Akropola (1985) was a collection of gloomy post-industrial ballads, and Borghesia led the pack. They had a tendency to focus on…"},{"book":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","page":291,"text":"…music, often indebted towards the local folk traditions and often looking towards the avantgarde. Heirs to the \"expressionistic\" school created by Laibach and Autopsia were Uz Isme Doma in the Czech Republic, Transmisia in Croatia, and Der Blutharsch in Switzerland. In Australia, Darrin Verhagen's…"}],"podcast":[{"kind":"podcast","title":"Presence in Industrial Scene","body":"Laibach was identified as one of the groups that people started to notice as the industrial scene rapidly grew in the mid-to-late 1980s. \"Plus, people began to pick up on groups with names like Frontline Assembly, Pancao, Psychic TV, Liebach, and many others.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source